// decisions.c4 — task-10 design decisions (DD-001..DD-004).
// Full prose survives in ../design-decisions/*.md; this is the reviewable shape.

model {
  dd-001 = decision 'DD-001: In-memory ssh tail relay, host stamped at ingestion' {
    description '''
      **Context:** hook telemetry only ever reaches the app through the local
      `~/.sich/events.jsonl` tail. A remote host writes its own sink and the payload
      carries **no hostname field**, so provenance exists for exactly one instant.

      **Decision:** a per-host `ssh ... tail -n 200 -F` child, read in memory, stamped
      with `sich_host` at ingestion and fed straight to the normalizer. Remote lines
      are never appended to the local sink.
    '''
    metadata {
      status 'accepted'
      rationale '''
        Host provenance is capturable only at ingestion, and merging into the local
        file is lossy and irreversible. Rotation ownership DISSOLVES under this option
        instead of needing a new owner: the local file keeps one writer and one rotator.
        The durability argument for appending locally is not real — the app IS the
        tailer, so lines that "survive locally" are lines that were never received.
        The durable store is the remote sink, which the remote forwarder rotates.
      '''
      alternatives '''
        Option 1 append into the local events.jsonl — rejected (discards host, creates
        a rotation-ownership problem that does not otherwise exist);
        Option 3 reverse tunnel + local listener — rejected (reintroduces the listener
        task-03 deliberately removed);
        Option 4 sshfs / network mount — rejected (discards host, new mount dependency)
      '''
      consequences '''
        positive: no listener, no port, no new remote binary; the ssh child liveness IS
        the transport liveness, so US-007 gets a real trigger for free;
        negative: nothing is buffered on the laptop if the app is down — history lives
        on the remote and is recovered by the bounded `-n 200` replay;
        decided parameters: down trigger = ssh child exit OR keepalive failure
        (ServerAliveInterval=15 x ServerAliveCountMax=3, ~45s worst case; immediate on
        exit); N (line-silence threshold) RETIRED, deliberately not used; X = card
        reflects it within 5s (one board poll); down state = distinct `unreachable`,
        never running/waiting; replay window = tail -n 200 -F; dedup = content-based,
        never a byte cursor
      '''
      stories 'US-003; US-007; US-008'
    }
  }

  dd-002 = decision 'DD-002: Carry SICH_CARD_ID on the pane, and set the tmux session env' {
    description '''
      **Context:** a relayed line must land on the card it belongs to. A launch command
      sets one shell process env, not the tmux session env, so a pane the operator opens
      later would be unbound while the terminal works fine.

      **Decision:** carry `SICH_CARD_ID` in the pane launch command exactly as the local
      path does, AND write it to the tmux session environment with `set-environment` at
      create, so later panes inherit it — plus make `(host, sanitized session name)`
      unique per card with a card-derived default name.
    '''
    metadata {
      status 'accepted'
      rationale '''
        Reuses the proven local mechanism: the whole read side (forwarder splice,
        boundCardID, the applySessions predicate) is untouched, so remote stops being a
        special case. The research finding that made env look disqualified was
        mis-scoped — inheritance is stale ONLY under name reuse, and name reuse was an
        unrelated card-modeling bug that needed fixing anyway. Fixing the modeling bug
        and fixing identity are the same act. `set-environment` needs no tmux version
        floor, which is decisive given a "both/varies" remote OS.
      '''
      alternatives '''
        Option 1 derive identity from the tmux session name at hook time — rejected
        ($TMUX-in-hook is unverified external behaviour needing a spike, and it would
        force the read-side rewrite this option avoids);
        Option 3 `new-session -e VAR=` — rejected (requires tmux >= 3.2);
        Option 5 ssh SendEnv/AcceptEnv — rejected (fails silently, which is the enemy);
        Option 4 card-unique names — ADOPTED as a companion, not an alternative
      '''
      consequences '''
        positive: zero read-side change; remote and local identity are one concept;
        negative: `-A` attaching a session Sich did not create still ignores the command
        argument, so `set-environment` never runs — probe for it and surface the
        collision rather than binding to nothing
      '''
      stories 'US-004'
    }
  }

  dd-003 = decision 'DD-003: Persist the remote spec (not the argv), no version bump' {
    description '''
      **Context:** a restored remote card comes back as a local shell, because the seed
      persists `user@host` as the card directory and the directory-exists check falls
      back to $HOME.

      **Decision:** option 2b — persist `RemoteSpecSnapshot {host, user?, sessionName,
      port?, identityFile?, remoteDir?}` on the existing PaneSnapshot as an optional
      field (no version bump), rebuild the argv through the same constructor, and
      host-qualify the recents store. The full SessionLocation enum is deferred.
    '''
    metadata {
      status 'accepted'
      rationale '''
        The invariant this codebase relies on is a property of the FILE FORMAT, not of
        the restore function: a format that cannot name a command cannot smuggle one.
        Storing an argv would relocate that guarantee into a code path DD-004 has to
        defend forever. When a structural guarantee and a coding guarantee cost nearly
        the same, take the structural one. The scope answer (a recents list against 2-5
        hosts, not a tree browser) removed the need the enum was sized for, and the risk
        budget belongs on DD-001.
      '''
      alternatives '''
        Option 1 full SessionLocation enum threaded through BoardCard/BoardSeed/
        SessionRegistry/PaneSnapshot/PaletteProject/recents — deferred, NOT discounted;
        Option 2a persist the raw argv — rejected in review (moves a structural
        guarantee into code);
        Option 3 treat remote cards as ephemeral — rejected (this is the story)
      '''
      consequences '''
        positive: small struct, existing decode path, no pane-discarding version bump
        (a version mismatch is IGNORED, not migrated);
        negative, stated plainly: resolveCwd keeps its local-path assumption as a silent
        $HOME fallback rather than a compile error — which is exactly how the shipped bug
        happened. Bought down with a regression test rather than types, and this task
        ADDS call sites written against `projectDir: String`, so promoting later is
        strictly MORE expensive, not less. A deferral, not a discount.
      '''
      stories 'US-006; US-009'
    }
  }

  dd-004 = decision 'DD-004: Structural ssh constraint, with a labelled best-effort scan' {
    description '''
      **Context:** "make `claude -p` impossible on a remote host" is not achievable — it
      is a string parsed by a shell we do not control. The achievable goal is that the
      app never GENERATES headless work.

      **Decision:** option 3 as the guarantee — ssh argvs may only be generated from
      constrained specs, never supplied, with the input constraints treated as part of
      the decision — plus option 1 scan underneath, documented in source as
      **best-effort and deliberately not sound**.
    '''
    metadata {
      status 'accepted'
      rationale '''
        Make the bad state unrepresentable rather than detected — the same means the
        codebase already uses at the PoolFirewall chokepoint and in the persisted
        snapshot shape. Precisely: the remote-command POSITION is not caller data
        (quoting closes it); the DESTINATION position IS caller data, and this DD
        constrains it. An earlier draft said the remote command is never caller data
        "so there is nothing to sanitize" — the exact sentence a future reader would
        cite to skip the destination guard. Option 2 (refuse any ssh) is right about
        direction and wrong about granularity: option 3 is option 2 with a closed
        allowlist instead of a closed door.
      '''
      alternatives '''
        Option 1 scan the ssh remote command for headless flags — RETAINED underneath,
        never instead of, and labelled in-code as not sound;
        Option 2 refuse any ssh argv on a spawn path — rejected (kills the feature)
      '''
      consequences '''
        positive: three new ssh-emitting call sites (relay, install/probe, restore) are
        constrained by construction, including the one that runs at launch with no human
        present;
        negative: the scan can never be sound, and the real failure mode is a future
        reader ASSUMING it is — hence the mandatory in-source label;
        implementation mandates: validate the composed destination POST-TRIM on the exact
        token appended; accept bracketed IPv6; reject a leading `-`; add
        Invalid.invalidDestination; delete the dead `var target = spec.target` store;
        parse() keeps its Optional return; match on tokenized argument boundaries, never
        a substring search for "claude"; record -p / -i emission order as a constraint
      '''
      stories 'US-001; US-003; US-005; US-006'
    }
  }

  dd-001 -> sys.relay 'shapes'
  dd-001 -> sys.host_state 'gives the down trigger'
  dd-002 -> sys.ssh_cmd 'adds set-environment + card-unique names'
  dd-002 -> sys.sessions 'leaves the read side untouched'
  dd-003 -> sys.registry 'persists the spec'
  dd-003 -> sys.palette 'host-qualifies recents'
  dd-004 -> sys.firewall 'adds the labelled ssh scan'
  dd-004 -> sys.ssh_cmd 'constrains the destination'
}

views {
  view decisions {
    title 'task-10: design decisions'
    include dd-001, dd-002, dd-003, dd-004
    include sys.relay, sys.host_state, sys.ssh_cmd, sys.firewall, sys.registry,
      sys.palette, sys.sessions
  }
}
